WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.190
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Xiaowen.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.268
XIAOWEN XIN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:03.268 --> 00:00:05.310
MARTIN OMANDER: You're a
product manager focusing

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.010
on serverless security, right?

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.053
XIAOWEN XIN: Yes.

00:00:09.053 --> 00:00:11.220
MARTIN OMANDER: Great,
because I have some developer

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.540
questions for you about
software supply chain security.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.300
XIAOWEN XIN: Great to hear, and
it's one of my favorite topics.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.674
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.943 --> 00:00:29.360
MARTIN OMANDER:
So Xiaowen, I hear

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.068
from developers who
are worried that they

00:00:31.068 --> 00:00:34.010
might be vulnerable to
software supply chain attacks.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.710
In the recent SolarWinds
attack, the attacker

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:40.790
compromised the build platform,
and in the Codecov attack,

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.130
the attacker uploaded
an artifact that was not

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.410
built by the CI/CD system.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.490
How can I protect my system?

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.910
XIAOWEN XIN: Well, Martin,
it's a complex problem,

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.700
and we want to help
developers navigate it.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.300
One thing you can do today is
to use Binary Authorization

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.180
to make sure that only
approved builds can

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.060
get deployed to Google
Kubernetes Engine or Cloud

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.915
Run in your project.

00:01:04.915 --> 00:01:07.290
MARTIN OMANDER: Great, because
I have a Cloud Run service

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.630
here that I'd like to
secure so that no one can

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.270
bypass my CI/CD pipeline.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.740
Can you walk me through it?

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.490
XIAOWEN XIN: Sure.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:18.160
And I know that you use Cloud
Build for your CI/CD pipeline,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.050
so let's first enable Binary
Authorization on your project.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:26.640
You go into the Cloud Console
and click the hamburger menu.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.740
Then select Security and
Binary Authorization.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.500
Click Enable to turn on the API.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.592
Now let's deploy your
Cloud Run service again.

00:01:37.592 --> 00:01:38.550
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, OK.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.260
Doing so now.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.535
This will also take a minute.

00:01:41.535 --> 00:01:43.410
XIAOWEN XIN: And now
that it's been deployed,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.840
let's go back to Binary
Authorization and click

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.100
Attestors.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.880
You will see that an attestor
has been automatically created.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.230
From now on, this attestor will
cryptographically sign every

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.890
build done in Cloud Build so
we know where it comes from.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.070
The signed attestation includes
provenance information,

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.380
such as the source code
that it was built from

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.974
and the commands
used to build it.

00:02:06.974 --> 00:02:07.770
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.585
And what will we do
with this attestor?

00:02:10.585 --> 00:02:11.710
XIAOWEN XIN: Good question.

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.010
If we go back to
the Policy tab, we

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.080
see that there is a
default policy here

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.780
that allows all
images to be deployed.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.000
Let's update this
policy to require

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.970
that all builds must be
signed by that attestor

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.590
before they can be deployed.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.950
And in other words, all builds
must come from your Cloud Build

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:29.970
pipeline.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:35.880
Click Edit Policy, select
Require Attestations,

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:41.190
click Add Attestors, and
in the Attestor name box,

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.500
you can select
built-by-cloud-build

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.520
in the dropdown.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.940
Then click to add this attestor.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.490
Click Save Policy.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.680
Now let's review the
policy again and confirm

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.530
that we're requiring images to
be approved by this attestor,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.490
meaning that all images
must come from Cloud Build.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.120
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, OK.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.160
Is this policy now being
enforced on my service?

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.570
XIAOWEN XIN: Not just yet.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.930
Right now, any authenticated
user with the right IAM

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:12.260
permissions can still deploy
images and containers,

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.540
regardless of where
they were built.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.883
MARTIN OMANDER: How
do I lock that down?

00:03:16.883 --> 00:03:19.050
XIAOWEN XIN: Let's go back
to your Cloud Run service

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.120
and click the Details tab.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.320
Where it says Binary
Authorization, click Enable.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.270
Now the policy is
guarding this service

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:30.233
from containers built
outside your project.

00:03:30.233 --> 00:03:31.650
MARTIN OMANDER: I
feel better now.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.390
If I tried to deploy
a container that

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.390
wasn't built by Cloud
Build in this project,

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.550
will the system
refuse to deploy it?

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.300
XIAOWEN XIN: Yeah.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.140
Try it out.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.218
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, I
will go here to Cloud Run.

00:03:43.218 --> 00:03:44.760
I happen to know
that Google provides

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.730
a sample container for testing.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.480
I don't know how that
container was built,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.490
but I do know that
it wasn't built

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.350
by Cloud Build in my project.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:55.212
Let's try to deploy it.

00:03:55.212 --> 00:03:57.420
XIAOWEN XIN: And it looks
like the deployment failed.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.670
MARTIN OMANDER: Perfect,
just what I want.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.130
Containers built in my
project can be deployed,

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.780
but not containers
built by others.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.720
Now what if I forget
to turn on Binary

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.460
Authorization for my service?

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.160
Is there a way to make sure that
Binary Authorization protects

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.980
all the services in my project?

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.320
XIAOWEN XIN: Yes,
glad you asked.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.240
We support an
organization policy

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.709
for Binary Authorization.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.086
Let's go turn that on.

00:04:22.086 --> 00:04:27.380
Click the hamburger menu, then
IAM &amp; Admin, then Organization

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.790
Policies.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:31.400
Select Allowed
Binary Authorization

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.070
Policies for Cloud Run.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:36.380
Then click Edit.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:39.350
And let's customize this
policy and add a rule that

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:42.650
allows the default policy.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.580
Click Done, then Save.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.100
This will take effect
within a few minutes.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.590
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
that was also easy.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.340
XIAOWEN XIN: Yeah.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.290
And this protects
against attackers,

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:56.830
but it also helps you ensure
that your builds are consistent

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.020
and that no one in
your organization

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:01.960
accidentally deploys containers
built on their laptops

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.120
or on other places.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.680
MARTIN OMANDER: I like that.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.630
Now we've plugged one
hole here, and viewers

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:10.060
should be able to try this
at home pretty easily.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.040
If they set this up
today, their projects

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.410
would be a little bit safer.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.110
But Xiaowen, what is
the bigger picture here?

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:20.110
How can an organization get
up to speed on software supply

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.850
chain security overall?

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.350
XIAOWEN XIN: Great question.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.330
Google and the
broader community have

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.640
launched a website
called Supply Chain

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.040
Levels for Software Artifacts.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.240
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
that's a mouthful.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.660
XIAOWEN XIN: It's
abbreviated to SLSA,

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.620
which is pronounced salsa, and
the website is at slsa.dev.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:42.230
On that website, you can
learn about a framework

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:44.640
for assessing the security
of your supply chain

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.150
and gradually increasing
that security level.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.460
We welcome everyone
to work with us

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:52.490
to level up the supply chain
security for the whole software

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.050
ecosystem.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:56.330
And in fact, what we
did together today

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:59.090
with Cloud Build and Binary
Authorization already

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.487
meets quite a few of
those requirements.

00:06:01.487 --> 00:06:02.570
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.520
Thank you for
joining me, Xiaowen.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:05.870
XIAOWEN XIN: You're welcome.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.400
MARTIN OMANDER: And thank
you, everyone, for watching.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.860
If you have
questions for Xiaowen

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.070
or me about Binary Authorization
or feedback on this episode,

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.760
post them in the comments.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.330
Also, please let us know what
other serverless topics you'd

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.020
like to hear about on the show.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.120
Bye for now.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

